WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
This is one of the many
videos that arose

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:06.363
out of my research for my new book,
How Not to Age.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.040
In this video and the next,

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.020
we're going to be talking about
the amount of influence

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.600
genetics has on longevity.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.980
"Is Longevity Genetic?"

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.860
It has long been said that
the best hope for a long life

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.440
is to choose your parents
wisely.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.980
After all, doesn't longevity
just run in the family?

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.240
Centenarians are people
who live to be at least 100,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.240
and their siblings
are certainly more likely

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:37.860
to become centenarians
themselves,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.200
and the parents of centenarians

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.300
have been found to be
more likely to have lived

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.620
to at least 90 years old.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.120
On the other hand,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.200
the lifespans of spouses
sometimes correlate as much,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.140
or even more than,
those of genetic relatives.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.600
Your partner may have as much
of an impact as your parent.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.920
After all, genes aren't the only
things that get passed down.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.080
Perhaps Grandma's
healthy recipes

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.420
or even a lifelong
love of running

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.460
runs in the family, too.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.980
To tease out
the role of genetics,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.260
researchers turn
to twin studies,

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.640
comparing difference between

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.640
identical twins
and fraternal twins.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.180
For example, imagine
you're trying to determine

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.980
the role of genetics
in cancer--

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.840
the role played by genes,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.420
versus other factors
that we may have control over.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.980
Since identical twins share
100 percent of their genes,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.130
whereas, on average, regular
twins only share 50 percent,

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.900
if genes play a large role,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.020
then you'd expect that identical
twins would be more likely

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.540
to share the same fate
than fraternal twins, right?

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.360
If there were no difference
in the chances

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.220
that both sets of twins
got the same disease,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.760
then it would appear
there's no genetic contribution.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.600
It turns out that
the overwhelming contributor

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.000
to the causation of cancer
is not genetics,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.040
but rather,
what we're exposed to.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.980
Primary genetic factors
may only account

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.200
for 5 to 10 percent
of all cancers.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.240
The BRCA genes popularized
by Angelina Jolie, for example,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.960
may account for as little as
2 percent of breast cancers.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.900
If one identical twin
gets breast cancer,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.290
the likelihood the other
will too is only 13 percent,

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.380
despite having
essentially identical DNA.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.620
Now, that's higher
than shared rates

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.240
among nonidentical twin women,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.100
so there is a genetic component,

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.040
but genes only appear
to make a minor contribution

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.480
to cancer risk.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.940
This is consistent with
the rates of common cancers

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.720
profoundly differing by as much
as 200-fold around the world.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.840
What do twin studies have to say

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.640
about the heritability
of lifespan?

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.480
Based on a study
of thousands of twin pairs,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.160
the heritability of longevity

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.000
was 26 percent for men
and 23 percent for women.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.080
Subsequent twin studies have
arrived at a similar estimate:

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.500
approximately 25 percent
of our lifespan is determined

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.420
by our genetic differences,

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.640
which means
how we live our lives

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.920
may determine
the bulk of our destiny.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.740
Estimates using other methods

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.380
tend to fall in
the 15 to 30 percent range.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.620
For example, an analysis
of millions of family trees

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.000
from 86 million public profiles
in an online genealogy database

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.880
led to an estimate
of 16 percent,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.760
though due to so-called
"assortative mating,"

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.460
meaning the fact that
we tend to pair up with mates

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.740
similar to ourselves,
rather than at random,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.260
that may actually be
an overestimate.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.860
Chosen partners often have
similar lifestyles,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.900
so some of that
16 percent estimate

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.300
may have been influenced
by families sharing

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.860
similar diets
and healthy habits,

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.360
and not exclusively their genes.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.980
Taking that into account,

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.640
the actual heritability
of lifespan may even be

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.200
well below 10 percent.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.920
To leverage
the lifespan leeway we have

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.380
beyond the relatively small
genetic component,

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.260
we must first understand
the aging pathways

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.380
that account for
the nine hallmarks of aging.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.900
The term "anti-aging" has been
much abused in popular culture,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:10.980
attached to all manner

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.660
of unproven products
and procedures.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.840
The term should probably
be reserved for things

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.140
that can delay or reverse aging
through the targeting

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.960
of one or more of the
established aging mechanisms.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.440
In a landmark paper
cited nearly 7,000 times

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.700
in the biomedical literature,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.560
"The Hallmarks of Aging"

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.900
delineates nine
common denominators

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.160
of the aging process.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.440
They are Genomic Instability,
the accumulation of DNA damage.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.940
Telomere Attrition,

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.480
the loss of the protective caps
at the end of our DNA strands.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.520
Epigenetic Alterations,

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.040
changes in the way
our genes are expressed.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.200
Loss of Proteostasis,

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.120
the buildup
of misfolded proteins.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.880
Deregulated Nutrient Sensing,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.300
metabolic alterations
particularly sensitive to diet.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.340
Mitochondrial Dysfunction,

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.060
the declining efficiency
of our cellular power plants,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.440
Cellular Senescence,
the arrest of cell replication.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.420
Stem Cell Exhaustion,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.900
the loss of the potential
of our tissues to regenerate,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:16.140
and Altered
Intercellular Communication,

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.780
the rise in
proinflammatory signals.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.760
I'm going to be
covering each of them,

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.400
and what we can do to slow,
stop, or reverse each one,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.160
in my upcoming book,
How Not to Age.

